So in those cases, it's okay, but it's really the situations that are uncontrollable or that cause us a lot of distress that end up being most unhealthy for us. Now, APA's 2015 Stress in America survey tells us that money continues to be a top source of stress for Americans from all economic backgrounds. Now, however, in this last survey, you know, lower income households reported higher overall stress levels than those living in higher income households. Can you explain why we're seeing such a stress gap between lower and higher income families? So there are a number of factors that contribute to higher levels of stress in people with lower

6
00:03:35.000 --> 00:04:02.000
incomes. First, as we know from the stress in America survey, financial stress is a very common cause of stress overall, and it's more likely to occur if, people have lower incomes. But people with lower incomes may also experience exposure to stress across many other domains in their lives. They may work in jobs that are stressful because they are demanding but don't allow a lot of control or their work environments may be unhealthy, for example. They could have a lot of noise or exposure to toxins in their work environment.

7
